<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Science and Learning</h1><p class="path-tagline">Libraries, translators and observatories: where empires invested in knowledge.</p><p class="path-intro">Imperial wealth has often paid for knowledge. Kings and caliphs founded libraries, endowed colleges, sponsored translations and built observatories, sometimes for prestige, sometimes for practical ends such as calendars and administration. This path visits the scholars of Ptolemaic Alexandria, the mathematicians of Gupta India, the translators of Abbasid Baghdad, the calendar reformers of the Seljuk court, the inventors of Song China and Ulugh Beg's astronomers in Samarkand, tracing how ideas passed from one centre of learning to the next.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/euclid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 323 BCE – 30 BCE</span><span class="path-step-name">Ptolemaic Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Ptolemaic Alexandria drew scholars such as Euclid, Eratosthenes and Herophilus to its Library and Mouseion.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Institution</span><span class="path-step-name">Library of Alexandria</span><span class="path-step-why">The great royal library at the heart of Ptolemaic scholarship, whose reputation has shaped the ideal of a universal library ever since.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/gupta-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abhijnanasakuntalam.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 240 CE – 550 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Gupta Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">India's Golden Age produced Kalidasa's poetry and Aryabhata's astronomy, built on the place-value decimal system later transmitted to Europe.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-kitab-al-mukhtasar-fi-hisab-al-jabr-wal-muqabala.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · 750 CE – 1517 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Abbasid Caliphate</span><span class="path-step-why">From Baghdad, founded in 762, the Abbasids presided over a cosmopolitan court where al-Khwarizmi wrote his treatise on al-jabr.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Encyclopedia · Institution</span><span class="path-step-name">House of Wisdom</span><span class="path-step-why">The centre that translated Greek, Persian, Indian and Syriac texts into Arabic, preserving Aristotle, Euclid, Galen and Ptolemy.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/omar-khayyam"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/omar-khayyam.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Person</span><span class="path-step-name">Omar Khayyam</span><span class="path-step-why">Poet and mathematician who, under Seljuk patronage, produced the Jalali calendar of 1079, more accurate than the Julian calendar.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/song-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bi-sheng.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 960 CE – 1279 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Song Dynasty</span><span class="path-step-why">Song China issued state paper money from 1024, first recorded the gunpowder formula in 1044 and chose officials by written examination.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/timurid-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamal-ud-din-behzad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Profile · 1363 CE – 1507 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Timurid Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Rebuilt with the plunder of Timur's conquests, Samarkand became under Shah Rukh and Ulugh Beg a genuine centre of intellectual life.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg-observatory"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg-observatory.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Institution</span><span class="path-step-name">Ulugh Beg Observatory</span><span class="path-step-why">Built in the 1420s, its tables listed more than a thousand stars and were later published at Oxford in 1665.</span></span></a></li></ol>
